diff --git a/assets/audio/README.md b/assets/audio/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..caadb94
--- /dev/null
+++ b/assets/audio/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,74 @@
+# assets/audio — the drop-in SFX contract (story 7.2, ODN-15)
+
+This directory is the **asset-drop contract** for the game's audio alerts.
+The game ships with **procedurally generated placeholder tones** (synthesized
+in `app/audio/audio.odin`) so the audio layer works with zero assets. Dropping
+real files here replaces the placeholders **slot by slot** — no code change,
+no re-bless of goldens (the cosmetic draw pattern is asset-independent).
+
+## Slots
+
+| Slot path | Alert | Replaces |
+|---|---|---|
+| `assets/audio/crisis_01.ogg` | crisis-alert sting, variant 1 | beep alarm #1 (3 beeps) |
+| `assets/audio/crisis_02.ogg` | crisis-alert sting, variant 2 | beep alarm #2 (2 beeps) |
+| `assets/audio/crisis_03.ogg` | crisis-alert sting, variant 3 | beep alarm #3 (4 beeps) |
+| `assets/audio/arrival_01.ogg` | arrival click, variant 1 | 1.8→2.4 kHz tick |
+| `assets/audio/arrival_02.ogg` | arrival click, variant 2 | 1.5→2.0 kHz tick |
+| `assets/audio/arrival_03.ogg` | arrival click, variant 3 | 2.2→2.8 kHz tick |
+
+## Format
+
+- Any raylib audio format: **OGG Vorbis recommended** (WAV / MP3 / FLAC also
+  load).
+- **Stings:** 0.5–1.5 s, loud but not harsh (they sit on top of gameplay).
+  Aim for a distinctive 2–3 note alert — it is the "crisis!" voice.
+- **Clicks:** ≤ 100 ms, short and percussive. They fire up to ~10×/s at peak
+  (see the throttle), so they must sit **well under** the sting in volume and
+  length — a click that overstays its 100 ms becomes a buzz.
+- Loudness: the module applies a fixed volume per slot (sting 0.85, click
+  0.40); deliver files near full scale and let the module attenuate.
+
+## Sources (user ruling 2026-08-17, FINAL — packet-plumber-v2-7.2-audio-juice)
+
+- **SFX — the two stings in this contract (crisis alert + arrival click):
+  ElevenLabs.**
+- **Music / ambience: Suno — the user holds the top-tier (Premier)
+  subscription, so songs are user-owned with perpetual commercial rights
+  including video games.** Only if ever needed — NOT in MVP scope.
+  **ElevenLabs Music is set aside** (the Studio Games exclusion — no
+  suitable tier for a commercial multi-platform game). The soundtrack is a
+  candidate parallel DistroKid release (owned IP); nothing here blocks on it.
+
+## Terms notes (user relay 2026-08-17)
+
+- (a) **Paid-plan ElevenLabs outputs are assigned to the user with perpetual
+  commercial rights, surviving subscription end.**
+- (b) **ElevenLabs SFX outputs are sublicensed to other ElevenLabs users BY
+  DEFAULT** — the **Disable** toggle on the Sound Effects product page opts
+  out. TODO when real SFX are generated: flip Disable for the shipped stings
+  (before they ship anywhere public).
+- (c) **Suno (Premier): user-owned songs, perpetual commercial rights incl.
+  video games.** (The earlier ElevenLabs-Music Studio Games tier note is
+  moot — ElevenLabs Music is set aside.)
+
+## Procedure
+
+1. Generate in **ElevenLabs** (the named SFX source for these stings) or your
+   editor of choice.
+2. Export to `assets/audio/<slot>` (see the table — names are `crisis_NN.ogg`
+   / `arrival_NN.ogg`, zero-padded).
+3. Launch the game: `odin run app`. A slot with a real file loads the file;
+   every other slot keeps its placeholder. A crisis trigger plays a random
+   (cosmetic-rng) sting variant; a packet delivery plays a throttled click
+   variant.
+4. Missing/partial sets are fine — each slot is independent.
+
+## Contract invariants (do not break)
+
+- The **variant count is fixed** (3 stings + 3 clicks): a slot always resolves
+  to *something* (file or placeholder). The cosmetic rng draw pattern never
+  depends on which slots are real — the `audio` T1 golden stays valid with or
+  without assets.
+- Never commit large binaries casually; the placeholders mean the repo needs
+  no audio assets to run every gate.
